## Use Case for eu-ehds:HealthDataAcessApplication
Description: This example illustrates the formal procedural entry point for secondary data reuse within the EHDS by 
focusing on eu-ehds:HealthDataAccessApplication. It focuses on a public health institute (the dpv:Applicant) which proposes a study to 
correlate urban atmospheric conditions with clinical respiratory outcomes. The eu-ehds:HealthDataAcessApplication represents the phase 
mandated by EHDS Article 67, where the applicant must justify the necessity, proportionality, and technical safety of their request before 
any data is released. The application is modeled as a specialised dpv:OrganisationalMeasure that serves as a structured compliance container. 
To satisfy Article 67(2)(f), the metadata explicitly describes the eu-ehds:EnvironmentalHealthDeterminant datasets (such as air quality sensor 
data) that the applicant intends to bring into the secure environment. Furthermore, the application fulfills the requirement of Article 67(2)(e) 
by providing a dpv:Justification for pseudonymisation, arguing that longitudinal patient tracking against daily environmental markers is 
impossible using anonymised data. As per Article 67(2)(c), the eu-ehds:HealthDataAcessApplication documents the expected benefit to public health, 
specifically how the findings will inform urban pollution mitigation strategies. Having captured these elements, the model distinguishes the 
application from an authorised eu-ehds:DataPermit, documenting the applicant's proposed dpv:Safeguard, their requested computing resources 
under Article 67(2)(i), and their activities, providing a transparent and auditable record for the Health Data Access Body (HDAB) to evaluate.
